{"id": "82abc10b-4a78-41b8-8c6a-66d0c0b41b7c", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T23:13:13.675902", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "c493b98e-0e5e-4f3b-b2e8-9d5f79fc00aa", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-d341e322-c8e0-40b7-b8a1-5d27e2b73719", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"echo \\\"--- system info ---\\\"; uname -a; echo \\\"--- python ---\\\"; python3 --version 2>&1; echo \\\"--- nproc ---\\\"; nproc 2>&1; echo \\\"--- memory ---\\\"; free -h 2>&1 | head -5\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.016, \"output\": \"--- system info ---\\nLinux d33eaef-lcedt 7.0.0-38-generic #38~24.04.4-Ubuntu SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Mon Sep 14 16:37:11 UTC 2 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux\\n--- python ---\\nPython 3.12.3\\n--- nproc ---\\n24\\n--- memory ---\\n               total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\\nMem:           125Gi        23Gi        10Gi       133Mi        92Gi       101Gi\\nSwap:           61Gi       2.3Gi        59Gi\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "48efae00-1a7e-4767-8288-b02543197458", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 9}
{"id": "66fc7a7e-50fc-4651-96c7-6ea4abb0ece2", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T23:14:02.581582", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "82abc10b-4a78-41b8-8c6a-66d0c0b41b7c", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-1beb60b7-5420-43d2-b30f-e8212e940710", "tool_call": {"id": "call-1beb60b7-5420-43d2-b30f-e8212e940710", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Propose experiment design for LLaMA-2 scaling question\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## Proposed Experiment Design (Plan)\\n\\n### 1. Research question, restated operationally\\nDoes model-size scaling from **llama-2-13b → llama-2-70b** still yield net performance gains once two orthogonal manipulations are introduced:\\n- (P) **Pre-training token budget** held artificially below the release reference, and\\n- (A) **Alignment regime** held below the release reference (no SFT, SFT-only, or partial RLHF)?\\n\\nWe want to estimate, with controlled alignment and pre-training budget, when (if ever) the 70B curve inverts or equals the 13B curve on (i) capability benchmarks, (ii) instruction-following, and (iii) safety/refusal behaviour.\\n\\n### 2. Design — controlled 2 × P × A factorial\\n\\n**Factor S — Model size (2 levels):** `S ∈ {13B, 70B}`. We use the LLaMA-2 architecture family and tokenizer; share everything except parameter count, depth, and width per the released configs.\\n\\n**Factor P — Pre-training token budget (3 levels):**\\n- `P_full` — released llama-2-{13b,70b} checkpoints (≈2T tokens, the canonical reference).\\n- `P_mid` — continued pre-training of the released base checkpoints on a held-out sub-corpus for **~200B tokens**. (Proxy for \\\"trained less\\\".)\\n- `P_low` — continued pre-training of the released base checkpoints for **~50B tokens**.\\n\\nTwo technical variants of the pre-training manipulation exist; I am **flagging this** for clarification (see Uncertainties):\\n- (a) *Data-truncation*: train from scratch (`P ∈ {50B, 200B, 2T}` tokens), enabling clean Compute-Optimal-style scaling curves but requiring enormous compute.\\n- (b) *Continued-training from release*: warm-start from released checkpoints and apply additional tokens. Cheap, faithful to released architecture, but introduces a small distribution-shift confound.\\n\\n**Factor A — Alignment regime (3 levels):**\\n- `A_base` — no fine-tuning; raw language model.\\n- `A_sft` — one supervised-fine-tuning pass on a fixed SFT mix (≤ the official recipe).\\n- `A_rlhf` — `A_sft` + RLHF with rejection sampling and one PPO iteration, using **a single shared reward model** trained once on a fixed preference mix.\\n\\n**Cells (2 sizes × 3 P × 3 A = 18).** Two directed contrasts carry most of the evidence:\\n| Contrast | What it tests |\\n|---|---|\\n| 70B@P_full@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_base | Pure pre-training-matched scaling effect. |\\n| 70B@P_low@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_base | Pure-size vs pure-data ceiling. |\\n| 70B@P_full@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_rlhf | Can alignment compensate for capability gap? |\\n| 70B@P_low@A_rlhf vs 13B@P_low@A_rlhf | Does scale still help when both are constrained? |\\n\\n### 3. Evaluation suites\\n\\n**A. Pre-training diagnostic (sanity):** held-out validation perplexity on a clean slice of the pre-training mix. Confirms the P manipulation succeeded and reveals whether the 70B diverges when under-trained (loss spikes, entropy collapse, instability).\\n\\n**B. Capability (knowledge, reasoning, code) — base-model-friendly, prompt-format neutral:**\\n- MMLU (5-shot, multiple-choice)\\n- ARC-Challenge (25-shot), ARC-Easy\\n- HellaSwag (10-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot)\\n- GSM8K (8-shot chain-of-thought, exact-match)\\n- HumanEval (pass@1 and pass@10)\\n- BoolQ, OpenBookQA\\n\\n**C. Alignment-sensitive (instruction-following & chat quality):**\\n- IFEval (strict and loose instruction-following accuracy)\\n- MT-Bench and AlpacaEval 2.0 (LC-winrate, length-controlled) — judge model fixed\\n- TruthfulQA (both MC and generation; separates knowledge from behaviour)\\n- BBQ (bias)\\n\\n**D. Safety / refusal calibration:**\\n- AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench — refusal rate vs compliance rate measured jointly\\n- Per-category breakdown; a single \\\"ASR\\\" metric is misleading without a refusal-rate denominator\\n\\nAll runs go through one harness (default: **lm-eval-harness** for MC/likelihood; a wrapper for generation benchmarks) and a fixed prompt-template per benchmark.\\n\\n### 4. Controls and confounds addressed\\n\\n- **Architecture & tokenizer**: identical LLaMA-2 family across sizes; shared 32k BPE tokenizer.\\n- **Pre-training data**: identical mixture at every level of P (only token count varies).\\n- **Reward model**: one RM trained once, reused across all RLHF cells.\\n- **SFT data and PPO hyper-parameters**: fixed per cell.\\n- **Prompt format / chat template**: fixed across sizes for any given A.\\n- **Decoding**: greedy (T=0) for MC / classification, fixed low-T sampling with documented `temperature`, `top_p`, `max_new_tokens` for generation. All cells identical.\\n- **Data contamination**: pre-training corpus is filtered against benchmark prompts where feasible; contamination audit reported.\\n- **Training stability**: monitor loss spikes / NaNs / entropy collapse; record and report as a co-variate — these are the dominant failure mode for under-trained large LMs.\\n- **Statistical variability**: ≥2 seeds for `A_sft` and ≥1 for `A_rlhf` (compute permitting); bootstrap 95% CIs on stochastic outputs.\\n- **Compute covariate**: report total GPU-hours per cell; it correlates with size, so we use it as a fixed covariate in regression, not as a “controlled” variable.\\n- **Judge model drift**: where LLM-as-judge is used (MT-Bench, AlpacaEval), the judge model and version are pinned.\\n\\n### 5. Analysis plan\\n\\n1. **Per-cell summary**: score table by (S, P, A) on every metric, with 95% CIs where applicable.\\n2. **Three-way decomposition**: linear model `score ~ S + P + A + (S×P) + (S×A) + (P×A) + (S×P×A)` per metric; report partial η² and significance. Where the metric is bounded/percentage, use beta regression or a stable transform.\\n3. **Directed contrasts** (the four in §2) with Bonferroni or Holm correction across the contrast family.\\n4. **Scaling-curve plots**: capability score vs P for each (S, A); overlay A=réglimes to show how the curve `70B − 13B` moves with alignment.\\n5. **Operating-point summary**: identify the regime where (i) 70B under regimes (P_low, A_base) **fails to beat** 13B@P_full@A_rlhf on capability *and* (ii) 70B@P_low@A_rlhf catches 13B@P_full@A_rlhf. Quantify.\\n6. **Robustness**: re-run key contrasts with two prompt-template choices and a different judge model.\\n\\n### 6. Feasibility note\\n\\n- **In this sandbox**: not executable. No GPU, no network, no datasets, no dependencies. The deliverable here is the design only; no run is claimed.\\n- **In a real environment**: full 2T-token pre-training of 70B from scratch is multi-million GPU-hours and far outside realistic academic budgets. The protocol therefore defaults to the **(b) Continued-training-from-release** variant for P, with **(a) Data-truncation** reserved for the 13B size if compute allows (Chinchilla-style scaling-curve support).\\n- Smaller A_rlhf cells (~1 PPO iteration on a fixed RM) are tractable on a single 8×H100 node for 13B and a 32×H100 node for 70B, depending on SFT-mix size.\\n- Datasets assumed obtainable: a public LLaMA-2-style SFT mix (e.g., UltraChat / Tulu / OpenAssistant) and a public preference mix (e.g., UltraFeedback / HH-RLHF) plus a public RM (e.g., Llama-3-OffsetBias or similar) — **all subject to researcher confirmation** (see Uncertainties).\\n\\n### 7. Assumptions (made by me, not in the task)\\n\\n1. The released `llama-2-13b` and `llama-2-70b` checkpoints are a faithful \\\"fully pre-trained\\\" reference at ≈2T tokens.\\n2. Continued pre-training from the release checkpoints is a valid, if approximate, stand-in for a from-scratch training run with the same token budget.\\n3. A behaviour gap on standard capability benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval) plus MT-Bench and IFEval is sufficient to answer the research question; no need for bespoke evaluations.\\n4. The 70B and 13B share SamLLaMA RM, SFT-data, and preference-data effects — i.e., alignment-quality differences across cells are driven by training procedure, not by data drift.\\n5. Same decoding settings across cells preserve the relative ordering of cells (a standard, but unverified, assumption).\\n6. Training instability (\\\"loss spikes\\\", \\\"entropy collapse\\\") is a confound for \\\"capability\\\" measurements and must be reported alongside capability scores, not hidden.\\n\\n### 8. Uncertainties — flagging for researcher clarification\\n\\n- **Compute budget**: which P-budgets are realistic? Three budgets on every size? Just (P_low, P_full) for 70B?\\n- **Pre-training corpus**: original LLaMA-2 mix (not publicly distributable) vs a public proxy (e.g., RedPajama v1)? Continued-training vs from-scratch?\\n- **Alignment data and reward model**: which SFT mix and which preference mix? Use a public RM or train a new one?\\n- **Evaluation harness**: lm-eval-harness, HELM, or OpenCompass? Generation benchmarks often diverge across harnesses.\\n- **Decoding settings**: greedy-only reporting, or sampling averaged over seeds at fixed (T, top_p)? pass@1 vs pass@10 for HumanEval.\\n- **Hardware availability** for the actual run: how many GPUs, what interconnect, what wall-clock budget? Affects whether A_rlhf can run more than one PPO iteration on 70B.\\n- **Definition of \\\"under-trained\\\"**: too-few tokens, too-few steps at the same LR schedule, or both? Affects the manipulation.\\n- **Definition of \\\"under-aligned\\\"**: absence of SFT? SFT-only? RLHF with a deliberately weaker RM?\\n- **Primary metric**: should the headline be a single aggregate (e.g., a fixed-weight composite of MMLU + GSM8K + MT-Bench + IFEval) or per-benchmark reporting?\\n\\n### 9. Contract confirmation\\n\\nThis is a **design-only deliverable**. No experiment is executed in this environment. No execution is claimed. The repository contains only `task.json`; no run was started.\",\"evidence\":\"Repository `/workspace` was inspected: it contains a single file, `task.json`, with the question and the explicit note \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No `track.md`, source code, datasets, or scripts are present. The environment has no GPU, no network, and no preinstalled dependencies, which is consistent with a design-only deliverable. No execution was attempted.\",\"assumptions\":\"1. The released llama-2-{13b,70b} checkpoints serve as the \\\"fully pre-trained\\\" reference at ~2T tokens. 2. Continued pre-training from release checkpoints is an acceptable proxy for \\\"less pre-training\\\" when from-scratch run-once-only is infeasible. 3. Public benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, IFEval, MT-Bench, AdvBench) are sufficient to detect the relevant capability, instruction-following, and safety differences. 4. A single shared RM, SFT mix and preference mix are used across all alignment cells so that A reflects procedure only. 5. Identical decoding settings preserve the relative ordering of cells. 6. Training instability (loss spikes, entropy collapse) is treated as a confound and reported. 7. Sizing (13B/70B) and token budget (P) can be treated as approximately independent factors once the data mixture is held constant.\",\"uncertainties\":[\"Compute budget for the actual (out-of-sandbox) run\",\"Pre-training corpus choice (original LLaMA-2 mix vs a public proxy like RedPajama v1), and pre-training manipulation (continued training vs data-truncation from scratch)\",\"SFT mix, preference mix, and reward model selection\",\"Evaluation harness (lm-eval-harness vs HELM vs OpenCompass) and prompt-template policy\",\"Decoding settings — greedy vs sampling, pass@k, seed averaging\",\"Hardware availability — number of GPUs, interconnect, wall-clock budget; affects RLHF iteration counts on 70B\",\"Definition of 'under-trained' (too-few tokens vs too-few steps vs both)\",\"Definition of 'under-aligned' (no SFT, SFT-only, RLHF with weaker RM, fewer PPO iterations)\",\"Primary metric: a single aggregate composite vs per-benchmark reporting\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-077a2213-f405-417a-a7fc-1bcecd1134fb", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 10}
