{"id": "f4a4e941-55ed-4788-bdba-6049e67e2fdd", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T23:14:27.126882", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "043cfa12-128b-4404-8faf-6e7b7c6f6379", "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "## Proposed Experiment Design (Plan)\n\n### 1. Research question, restated operationally\nDoes scaling from **llama-2-13b → llama-2-70b** still yield net performance gains once two orthogonal manipulations are introduced:\n- (P) **Pre-training token budget** held artificially below the release reference, and\n- (A) **Alignment regime** held below the release reference (no SFT, SFT-only, or partial RLHF)?\n\nWe want to estimate, with controlled alignment and pre-training budget, when (if ever) the 70B curve inverts or equals the 13B curve on (i) capability benchmarks, (ii) instruction-following, and (iii) safety/refusal behaviour.\n\n### 2. Design — controlled 2 × P × A factorial\n\n**Factor S — Model size (2 levels):** `S ∈ {13B, 70B}`. Use the LLaMA-2 architecture family and tokenizer; share everything except parameter count, depth, and width per the released configs.\n\n**Factor P — Pre-training token budget (3 levels):**\n- `P_full` — released llama-2-{13b,70b} checkpoints (≈2T tokens, the canonical reference)\n- `P_mid` — continued pre-training of the released base checkpoints on a held-out sub-corpus for **~200B tokens**\n- `P_low` — continued pre-training of the released base checkpoints for **~50B tokens**\n\nTwo technical variants of the pre-training manipulation exist; **flagging this** for clarification (see Uncertainties):\n- (a) *Data-truncation*: train from scratch with `P ∈ {50B, 200B, 2T}` tokens. Clean Compute-Optimal-style curves; very expensive.\n- (b) *Continued-training from release*: warm-start from released checkpoints and apply additional tokens. Cheap, faithful to released architecture, small distribution-shift confound.\n\n**Factor A — Alignment regime (3 levels):**\n- `A_base` — no fine-tuning; raw LM\n- `A_sft` — one supervised-fine-tuning pass on a fixed SFT mix (≤ the official recipe)\n- `A_rlhf` — `A_sft` + RLHF with rejection sampling + one PPO iteration, using **one shared reward model** trained once on a fixed preference mix\n\n**Cells (2 × 3 × 3 = 18).** Four directed contrasts carry most of the evidence:\n| Contrast | What it tests |\n|---|---|\n| 70B@P_full@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_base | Pure pre-training-matched scaling effect |\n| 70B@P_low@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_base | Pure size vs data ceiling |\n| 70B@P_full@A_base vs 13B@P_full@A_rlhf | Can alignment compensate for capability gap? |\n| 70B@P_low@A_rlhf vs 13B@P_low@A_rlhf | Does scale still help when both are constrained? |\n\n### 3. Evaluation suites\n\n**A. Pre-training diagnostic (sanity):** held-out validation perplexity on a clean slice of the pre-training mix. Confirms the P manipulation worked and exposes under-training failure modes (loss spikes, entropy collapse, instability).\n\n**B. Capability (knowledge, reasoning, code) — base-friendly, prompt-format neutral:**\n- MMLU (5-shot), ARC-Challenge (25-shot), ARC-Easy\n- HellaSwag (10-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot)\n- GSM8K (8-shot CoT, exact match)\n- HumanEval (pass@1, pass@10)\n- BoolQ, OpenBookQA\n\n**C. Alignment-sensitive (instruction-following & chat):**\n- IFEval (strict and loose)\n- MT-Bench and AlpacaEval 2.0 (LC-winrate, length-controlled) — judge model fixed\n- TruthfulQA (MC and generation)\n- BBQ (bias)\n\n**D. Safety / refusal calibration:**\n- AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench — joint refusal rate + attack-success-rate, per category\n\nAll runs through **one harness** (default: **lm-eval-harness** for MC/likelihood; a wrapper for generation), with one prompt template per benchmark.\n\n### 4. Controls and confounds\n\n- **Architecture & tokenizer**: identical LLaMA-2 family; shared 32k BPE tokenizer.\n- **Pre-training data**: identical mixture across all P levels; only token count varies.\n- **Reward model**: one RM, trained once, shared across all RLHF cells.\n- **SFT data and PPO hyper-parameters**: fixed per cell.\n- **Prompt template / chat template**: fixed across sizes for a given A.\n- **Decoding**: greedy (T=0) for MC / classification; fixed low-T sampling with documented `temperature`, `top_p`, `max_new_tokens` for generation. Identical across cells.\n- **Data contamination**: filter pre-training corpus against benchmark prompts where feasible; contamination audit reported.\n- **Training stability**: monitor loss spikes / NaNs / entropy collapse; record, report as co-variate — this is the dominant failure mode for under-trained large LMs.\n- **Statistical variability**: ≥2 seeds for `A_sft`; ≥1 for `A_rlhf` (compute-permitting); bootstrap 95% CIs on stochastic outputs.\n- **Compute covariate**: report GPU-hours per cell; use as a fixed covariate in regression rather than a \"controlled\" variable (correlates with size).\n- **Judge model drift**: where LLM-as-judge is used (MT-Bench, AlpacaEval), the judge model and version are pinned.\n\n### 5. Analysis plan\n\n1. **Per-cell summary table**: score by (S, P, A) on every metric, with 95% CI where relevant.\n2. **Three-way decomposition**: linear model `score ~ S + P + A + (S×P) + (S×A) + (P×A) + (S×P×A)` per metric; report partial η² and significance. Use beta regression or a stable transform on bounded percentages.\n3. **Directed contrasts** (the four in §2) with Holm/Bonferroni correction across the contrast family.\n4. **Scaling-curve plots**: capability score vs P for each (S, A); overlay alignment regimes to visualise how the curve `70B − 13B` shifts.\n5. **Operating-point summary**: identify the regime where (i) 70B@(P_low, A_base) **fails to beat** 13B@(P_full, A_rlhf) on capability *and* (ii) 70B@(P_low, A_rlhf) catches 13B@(P_full, A_rlhf). Quantify.\n6. **Robustness**: re-run key contrasts with two prompt templates and a different judge model.\n\n### 6. Feasibility note\n\n- **In this sandbox**: not executable. No GPU, no network, no datasets, no dependencies. The deliverable here is the design only; no run is claimed.\n- **In a real environment**: full 2T-token from-scratch pre-training of 70B is multi-million GPU-hours and out of scope for most academic budgets. The protocol therefore defaults to the **(b) Continued-training-from-release** variant for P on the 70B side. From-scratch (variant a) is feasible for the 13B side and useful for Chinchilla-style scaling-curve support.\n- Smaller A_rlhf cells are tractable on a single 8×H100 node for 13B and ≈32×H100 for 70B per PPO iteration, depending on SFT-mix size.\n- Datasets assumed obtainable: public SFT mix (UltraChat/Tulu/OpenAssistant), public preference mix (UltraFeedback/HH-RLHF), and a public RM — **all subject to researcher confirmation** (see Uncertainties).\n\n### 7. Contract confirmation\n\nThis is a **design-only deliverable**. No experiment is executed in this environment. No execution is claimed. The repository contains only `task.json`; no run was started.\n\n### 8. Open questions for the researcher\n\nThese double as design knobs I will lock in only after researcher approval:\n(1) Compute budget for the real run. (2) Pre-training corpus and manipulation style (continued vs from-scratch). (3) SFT mix, preference mix, and reward model. (4) Evaluation harness and prompt-template policy. (5) Decoding policy (greedy-only vs sampled). (6) Hardware (GPU count and wall-clock). (7) Operational definitions of \"under-trained\" and \"under-aligned\". (8) Primary metric: single composite vs per-benchmark.", "evidence": "Repository `/workspace` was inspected: it contains a single file, `task.json`, with the question and the explicit note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No `track.md`, source code, datasets, or scripts are present. The environment has no GPU, no network, and no preinstalled dependencies, consistent with a design-only deliverable. No execution was attempted.", "assumptions": ["The released llama-2-{13b,70b} checkpoints serve as the \"fully pre-trained\" reference at ~2T tokens.", "Continued pre-training from release checkpoints is an acceptable proxy for \"less pre-training\" when from-scratch run-once-only is infeasible.", "Public benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, HumanEval, IFEval, MT-Bench, AdvBench) are sufficient to detect the relevant capability, instruction-following, and safety differences.", "A single shared RM, SFT mix, and preference mix are used across all alignment cells so that A reflects training procedure only.", "Identical decoding settings preserve the relative ordering of cells (standard but unverified).", "Training instability (loss spikes, entropy collapse) is treated as a confound and reported alongside capability scores.", "Size (13B/70B) and token budget (P) can be treated as approximately independent factors once the data mixture is held constant."], "uncertainties": ["Compute budget for the actual (out-of-sandbox) run.", "Pre-training corpus choice (original LLaMA-2 mix vs a public proxy) and the manipulation style (continued training vs data-truncation from scratch).", "SFT mix, preference mix, and reward model selection.", "Evaluation harness (lm-eval-harness vs HELM vs OpenCompass) and prompt-template policy.", "Decoding policy — greedy vs sampling, pass@k, seed averaging.", "Hardware availability — number of GPUs, interconnect, wall-clock budget; affects RLHF iteration counts on 70B.", "Operational definition of 'under-trained' (too-few tokens vs too-few steps vs both).", "Operational definition of 'under-aligned' (no SFT vs SFT-only vs weaker RM vs fewer PPO iterations).", "Primary metric — single composite vs per-benchmark reporting."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-54511b63-7fdc-4007-b26e-f2167dfb44f7", "tool_call": {"id": "call-54511b63-7fdc-4007-b26e-f2167dfb44f7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Propose experiment design for LLaMA-2 scaling question\"