{"id": "1e4a0136-85ab-40dc-97b7-64cae483dd00", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T23:16:05.397384", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "998113b4-dfbc-43db-9f38-1959b83d31ec", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design (Design-Only Deliverable)\n\n## 0. Contract\n\nThis document is a design specification. **No experiment is executed in this environment and no results are claimed.** The runtime has no GPU, no network, and no project dependencies; the repository at `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (sha256 `4f5a54b3…2c36bf`). All cells exist in this document as configurations, not as measurements.\n\n## 1. Question and operational hypotheses\n\n**Q.** Does scaling `llama-2-13b → llama-2-70b` necessarily improve performance, or can **insufficient pre-training (P)** and **under-refined alignment (A)** negate the parameter advantage?\n\nWe frame the question as three falsifiable claims:\n\n- **H1 (capacity).** With matched pre-training budget *and* matched alignment regime, 70B outperforms 13B on every capability benchmark.\n- **H2 (pre-training dilution).** At a fixed alignment regime, reducing pre-training token budget below the 70B's compute-optimal frontier erodes the 70B advantage and may flip the ranking on hard-knowledge benchmarks.\n- **H3 (alignment parity).** With matched pre-training budget, an `A_rlhf` 13B can match or exceed a base `A_base` 70B on instruction-following. Combined with H2, this is the regime where size \"loses\".\n\nA 2 × 3 × 3 factorial with locked defaults provides a single design that can confirm, refute, or partially support each.\n\n## 2. Design — locked factorial\n\nIndependent variables and levels:\n\n| Factor | Levels | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **S — Size** | `13B`, `70B` | LLaMA-2 architecture, shared 32k BPE tokenizer |\n| **P — Pre-training token budget** | `50B`, `200B`, `2T` | Continued-training-from-release on 70B; from-scratch on 13B (Chinchilla bridging) |\n| **A — Alignment regime** | `A_base`, `A_sft`, `A_rlhf` | `A_base` is the base LM; `A_sft` is one SFT pass; `A_rlhf` is SFT + one PPO iteration with a frozen shared RM |\n\nCell count: 2 × 3 × 3 = **18 cells**.\n\nChinchilla (Hoffmann et al. 2022) compute-optimal anchor, characterizing each cell:\n\n- Chinchilla-optimal token budgets: ≈260B for 13B-style models; ≈1.4T for 70B-style models.\n- Per-cell position relative to the frontier (frontier ≈ N_params × 20 tokens, non-embedding params):\n\n| Cell | Tokens | Frontier (≈) | Position |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 13B@50B | 50B | ~260B | **far-below** (~0.19×) |\n| 13B@200B | 200B | ~260B | near (~0.77×) |\n| 13B@2T | 2T | ~260B | **far-above** (~7.7×) |\n| 70B@50B | 50B | ~1.4T | **far-below** (~0.036×, essentially untrained) |\n| 70B@200B | 200B | ~1.4T | **far-below** (~0.14×) |\n| 70B@2T | 2T | ~1.4T | near-to-above (~1.43×) |\n\nEach cell in the per-benchmark table in §6 carries this annotation. The reader can read off directly which cells sit *below* the compute-optimal frontier (i.e., token-limited) and which sit *above* (compute-limited / over-trained).\n\n## 3. Pre-training budgets — locked execution rule\n\n- **70B side (default): continued-training from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-hf`.** The release weights represent the 2T reference. For `P_low` (50B) and `P_mid` (200B) on the 70B side, continued pre-training *does not implement \"less pre-training\"*, because LLaMA-2 intermediates are not released. We therefore procure the closest open LLaMA-2-70B-equivalent with intermediates exposed — default candidates: **RedPajama-INCITE-70B-suite intermediates** if/where they exist, else **openLLaMA-70B-equivalent**. Discrepancy from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b` is documented as a known confound (§5).\n- **13B side (default): from-scratch at 50B / 200B / 2T tokens on the open LLaMA-2-13B config**, acting as the Chinchilla-style bridging run. This gives a clean scaling curve on the 13B side and isolates pre-training effect size.\n- **Mixture**: both sides train on **RedPajama-v2** (or, if the researcher prefers, **FineWeb-Edu**). Tokenizer is the LLaMA-2 32k BPE. The mixture slice is shared across all P cells; only token count varies.\n\nThis decision rule pre-empts the asymmetric-availability problem and makes the 13B curve the canonical reference for the scaling claim, with the 70B side using release-anchored substitution.\n\n## 4. Alignment regime — locked specification\n\n- **A_base**: raw base LM; no fine-tuning.\n- **A_sft**: one supervised-fine-tuning pass on a **fixed, frozen SFT mixture**. Default: **Tulu-2-SFT-mixture** (Allen AI) re-mixed with a **200k-dialogue UltraChat** subset, fixed proportion 60/40. Single SFT training run per (S, P) cell; **2 seeds** per cell.\n- **A_rlhf**: `A_sft` + **one** PPO iteration with rejection sampling. Default:\n  - Preference data: **UltraFeedback** (Cai et al. 2023, ~64k pairs), augmented with **HH-RLHF-Anthropic** (~170k pairs) at 60/40 mix. Frozen.\n  - Reward model: **one shared RM trained once** on the same preference mix, base = LLaMA-2-7B, fixed recipe. The RM is **frozen across all cells** so `A_rlhf` reflects only PPO procedure, not designer drift. Single reward head; no online distillation.\n  - RM is held fixed across all (S, P) cells, which is the explicit control requested.\n  - 1 seed per cell; **PPO policy-LR and KL coefficient are fixed constants**, identical across all cells.\n  - Number of PPO steps is held identical across cells; we **do not** add additional iterations to the 70B cells, which would conflate wall-clock with procedure.\n\nIf an off-the-shelf RM is preferred: **Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B** is the named fallback, with the caveat that it is not in the LLaMA-2 family (a documented distributional shift; we treat it as a sensitivity analysis, not the default).\n\n## 5. Controls and confounds — locked\n\n- **Architecture / tokenizer**: identical LLaMA-2 family across sizes; shared 32k BPE tokenizer.\n- **Pre-training data**: identical mixture across all P cells; only token count varies.\n- **Reward model**: one shared, frozen RM across all RLHF cells.\n- **SFT data and PPO hyper-parameters**: fixed per cell.\n- **Prompt template / chat template**: one fixed template per benchmark, and one chat template applied uniformly across sizes for any given A (per §6).\n- **Decoding (locked)**:\n  - MC / likelihood: **greedy** (temperature 0).\n  - Generation (HumanEval, GSM8K-CoT, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval, IFEval): single, documented config — `temperature = 0.7`, `top_p = 0.95`, `max_new_tokens` per task (256 HumanEval, 512 GSM8K, 1024 MT-Bench), `seed = 0`, fixed stop tokens per benchmark.\n  - All cells use the identical config.\n- **Contamination audit**: pre-training corpus filtered against benchmark prompts (n-gram match + minhash) where feasible; contamination report attached.\n- **Training instability**: monitor loss spikes / NaNs / entropy collapse throughout pre-training; report as a covariate. Under-trained 70B is expected to be the most unstable cell.\n- **Statistical variability**: ≥2 seeds for A_sft, ≥1 for A_rlhf per (S, P) cell; deterministic for A_base (the checkpoint is the seed).\n- **Bootstrap 95% CIs**: applied to all stochastic-output metrics (validation perplexity, HumanEval pass@k, GSM8K pass@1, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval LC, IFEval, refusals).\n- **Wilson CIs**: applied to bounded MC metrics (MMLU, ARC, etc.) where each question is a Bernoulli trial.\n- **Compute covariate**: GPU-hours per cell recorded; reported, not controlled (correlates with S).\n- **Chinchilla covariate**: token-to-frontier ratio per cell, reported in every table (see §2).\n- **Known unresolved confounds (acknowledged, not silenced)**:\n  70B `P_low` / `P_mid` use a continued-training or open-recipe substitute that is not byte-identical to `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b`; this is documented and analysed as a sensitivity check.\n\n## 6. Evaluation suite — locked harness, templates, decoding\n\n**Harness**: **lm-eval-harness** (EleutherAI), pinned at a specific git tag (default: `v0.4.5`). Generation benchmarks not covered by lm-eval-harness are run using a stable wrapper around `vllm`/`transformers` (pinned) that reuses the harness's prompt templates where possible.\n\n**One fixed prompt template per benchmark**, taken from the harness defaults and pinned. No benchmark-level template variations across cells.\n\n**Benchmarks**:\n\n| Block | Benchmark | Decoding | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Pre-training diag. | held-out validation perplexity (LLaMA-2-mixture slice) | n/a | sanity for P manipulation |\n| Capability | MMLU (5-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | knowledge |\n| Capability | ARC-Challenge (25-shot), ARC-Easy | greedy / loglikelihood | reasoning |\n| Capability | HellaSwag (10-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | commonsense |\n| Capability | WinoGrande (5-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | commonsense |\n| Capability | BoolQ, OpenBookQA | greedy / loglikelihood | reading |\n| Capability | GSM8K (8-shot CoT) | sampling (config above) | reasoning |\n| Capability | HumanEval (pass@1, pass@10) | sampling (config above) | code |\n| Instruction-following | IFEval (strict and loose) | sampling | instruction-following |\n| Chat quality | MT-Bench (single judge config) | sampling | chat |\n| Chat quality | AlpacaEval 2.0 LC-winrate | sampling + pinned judge | chat |\n| Honesty | TruthfulQA (MC + generation) | greedy / sampling | honesty |\n| Bias | BBQ | greedy | bias |\n| Safety | AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench | sampling | safety |\n\nJudge model for MT-Bench and AlpacaEval is **pinned and named** (default: `gpt-4-turbo-2024-04-09` for MT-Bench; same for AlpacaEval). Judge version is constant across all cells.\n\n## 7. Primary metric and reporting plan\n\nReporting is split by purpose to avoid spurious aggregation:\n\n- **Primary capability composite (CC)**: unweighted mean of standardised scores on MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, GSM8K, HumanEval pass@1. We standardise each metric against a fixed reference (release P_full@A_base 13B scores per-benchmark). Reported with bootstrap 95% CI.\n- **Per-benchmark table**: every benchmark listed in §6 reported cell-by-cell with 95% CI; **safety, instruction-following, and chat-quality are NOT blended into CC** and are reported in separate tables.\n- **Operating-point table**: each cell annotated with Chinchilla position (above / near / below frontier), inference compute (FLOPs/token), training compute (GPU-hours), and instability flags.\n- **Directed contrasts (§2)**: each reported with effect size and adjusted p-value (Holm across the four contrasts).\n\nThis satisfies the researcher's directive: \"per-benchmark table plus an unweighted capability composite, with safety and instruction-following reported separately rather than blended in.\"\n\n## 8. Analysis plan (frozen)\n\nFor each metric `y`:\n\n1. `y ~ S + P + A + S×P + S×A + P×A + S×P×A` linear (or beta-regression for bounded) model; report partial η² for each term.\n2. Compute the four contrasts from §2 with mean-difference estimators and bootstrap 95% CIs; Holm-correct across the four.\n3. Per-cell tables with CIs, Chinchilla tag, instability tag.\n4. Plots: capability metric vs P for each (S, A); overlay the four contrasts.\n5. Sensitivity check: rerun the four contrasts on the 70B side substituting the open-recipe weights if intermediates are not from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b`.\n\nPre-registered failure conditions:\n\n- If 70B@P_low@A_base is more unstable than `1 SD-loss-spike-rate` above 13B@P_low@A_base, the 70B@P_low cells are flagged unstable and reported separately. We do not silently drop them.\n- If contamination audit flags >1% benchmark prompt overlap in any cell's training data, that cell is excluded from CC; the conflict is reported.\n\n## 9. Feasibility and execution notes\n\n- **In this sandbox**: not executable. No GPU, no network, no datasets, no project dependencies. The deliverable here is the design only. No run is claimed; no fabricated numbers appear above. Every cell exists as a configuration.\n- **In a real environment**:\n  - 70B continued-training from release, 50B tokens: a single 32×H100 (80 GB) node × ~2–4 days (~3,000–6,000 GPU-hours).\n  - 70B continued-training from release, 200B tokens: ~12,000–24,000 GPU-hours.\n  - 70B SFT × 2 seeds: ~1,500 GPU-hours per (S, P) cell on the 70B side; double-counted for the 4 (P × 2-cell) primary 70B P sweep ⇒ a few thousand GPU-hours.\n  - 70B PPO × 1 iter (A_rlhf): ~2,000–5,000 GPU-hours per (S, P) cell.\n  - 13B from-scratch at 50B / 200B / 2T: dominated by 2T at ~80k–120k GPU-hours on 13B; the 2T run is the budget bottleneck on the 13B side. **Bridging alternative**: 13B from-scratch at 50B / 200B only, with 13B@2T supplied by `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-hf` and explicitly flagged as not-from-scratch (saves most of the cost). The 70B at \"2T\" is similarly anchored to the release weights and the same flag applies.\n- **Storage**: 70B FP16 ≈ 140 GB per checkpoint; 18 cells × 2 (best + last) ≈ 5 TB of checkpoints. Mitigation: keep best checkpoint per cell + final SFT/PPO iter only; intermediate training-state checkpoints are not retained.\n- **Inference compute for generation benchmarks**: roughly 1–2 GPU-day per cell on the 70B side at MT-Bench scale; negligible for likelihood benchmarks.\n\n## 10. Limitations explicitly flagged (not papered over)\n\n1. **Continued-training vs from-scratch asymmetry**: 13B is from-scratch; 70B is release-anchored. Discussed in §3 and §5.\n2. **Open-recipe substitute for 70B intermediates**: if RedPajama / openLLaMA 70B intermediates are used in lieu of LLaMA-2 intermediates, the curve is not strictly LLaMA-2. Sensitivity check defined in §8.\n3. **Single PPO iteration**: with one PPO step, `A_rlhf` cell differences are small in absolute terms; the design is sensitive to this. Documented as a deliberate choice to isolate the *procedure effect* rather than to chase state-of-the-art.\n4. **No closed-source data**: the design uses only public corpora and frozen open RM/SFT/RM-training recipes.\n5. **Compute bookkeeping**: GPU-hours is reported as a covariate, not an independent variable; size and compute remain partially confounded. The Chinchilla-relative annotation makes this visible.\n6. **Judge model drift**: MT-Bench/AlpacaEval numbers depend on the judge. We pin a single judge; absolute scores are not directly comparable to other studies, but the *relative* ordering across our 18 cells remains internal-valid.\n\n## 11. Bottom-line answers the design supports\n\nThe factorial is built to support a precise, falsifiable bottom-line readout:\n\n- *If `70B@P_full@A_base - 13B@P_full@A_base > 0` on CC* — capacity hypothesis (H1) partly held; inspect per-benchmark for where it fails.\n- *If `70B@P_low@A_base ≤ 13B@P_full@A_base` on hard-knowledge benchmarks* — pre-training dilution hypothesis (H2) supported.\n- *If `13B@P_full@A_rlhf - 70B@P_full@A_base > 0` on IFEval and MT-Bench* — alignment-parity hypothesis (H3) supported.\n- *If `70B@P_low@A_rlhf` matches `13B@P_full@A_base` on capability and beats it on instruction-following* — the most direct case for \"size loses\" when both factors are constrained.\n\nEach of these is a pre-registered success/failure criterion with a defined computed statistic.\n\n---\n\n*End of design. No experiments have been run. No execution is claimed.*\n", "evidence": "Repository `/workspace` contains a single file, `task.json` (sha256 `4f5a54b3501ea9039e8dd00c8cc49bb0a82e80eb1f79520fbbbb13b73e2c36bf`); no source code, datasets, scripts, or prior runs are present. The sandbox has no GPU, no network, and no preinstalled dependencies, consistent with the design-only contract. The environment was inspected via `ls -la /workspace` and `sha256sum /workspace/task.json`; no code execution related to the experiment was performed and no results are reported anywhere in this document.", "assumptions": ["The released llama-2-13b and llama-2-70b base checkpoints (≈2T tokens, Meta, 2023) serve as the canonical \"fully pre-trained\" reference for those sizes.", "Continued pre-training from the released llama-2-70b is an acceptable practical proxy for \"less pre-training\" on the 70B side, with the caveat documented in Limitations.", "From-scratch training of LLaMA-2-13B-style models at 50B, 200B, and 2T tokens on RedPajama-v2 or FineWeb-Edu is achievable and produces a clean Chinchilla bridging curve.", "Tulu-2-SFT-mixture + a 200k-dialogue UltraChat subset is a faithful stand-in for the LLaMA-2-Chat SFT data, and UltraFeedback + HH-RLHF (60/40) is a faithful stand-in for the LLaMA-2-Chat preference data.", "An RM trained once on UltraFeedback+HH-RLHF with a fixed recipe and frozen across all (S, P) cells adequately controls \"shared RM\" as required.", "lm-eval-harness (v0.4.5 default) with one fixed prompt template per benchmark, greedy decoding for likelihood and (temperature=0.7, top_p=0.95, seed=0) for generation, yields reliable cross-cell comparisons.", "Chinchilla's compute-optimal frontier (Hoffmann et al. 2022) at N_eff × 20 tokens is the right reference for \"above/below frontier\" annotation.", "One PPO iteration per (S, P) cell under a fixed KL coefficient and policy-LR captures the A_rlhf effect of interest; multi-iteration RLHF is treated as out-of-scope.", "The unweighted mean of standardised capability benchmarks is a defensible primary composite; safety and instruction-following are kept separate.", "Public benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, GSM8K, HumanEval, IFEval, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval 2.0, TruthfulQA, BBQ, AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench) adequately discriminate the relevant differences."], "uncertainties": ["Compute budget for the actual (out-of-environment) execution; specifically, whether the 13B@2T cell can really be done from-scratch or must be anchored to `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-hf`.", "Availability of LLaMA-2-class 70B intermediates from the RedPajama / openLLaMA / Together ecosystems; if not, the design sensitivity check (§8 step 5) is the only quantitative fallback.", "Best public surrogate for the LLaMA-2-Chat SFT mixture (Tulu-2 vs UltraChat vs OASST) and the proportion of synthetic vs human-curated data.", "RM choice — train-a-shared-RM-from-scratch vs use Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B as a frozen out-of-family RM.", "Judge model selection for MT-Bench / AlpacaEval and the licensing constraints on commercial judges in the actual execution environment.", "Whether the contamination audit (n-gram + minhash) at >1% threshold is the right operating point.", "Whether one PPO iteration is enough signal on the 70B side; if not, multi-iteration RLHF may bias A_rlhf in size-dependent ways.", "Boundary of \"near frontier\" in the Chinchilla annotation — currently ±25% subjectively; a precise rule can be added if the researcher specifies."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-5e02a21a-9e00-43e9-a7b2-53a8234325b8", "tool_call": {"id": "call-5e02a21a-9e00-43e9-a7b2-53a8234325b8", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Submit final experiment design with locked defaults\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design (Design-Only Deliverable)\\n\\n## 0. Contract\\n\\nThis document is a design specification. **No experiment is executed in this environment and no results are claimed.** The runtime has no GPU, no network, and no project dependencies; the repository at `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (sha256 `4f5a54b3\\u20262c36bf`). All cells exist in this document as configurations, not as measurements.\\n\\n## 1. Question and operational hypotheses\\n\\n**Q.** Does scaling `llama-2-13b \\u2192 llama-2-70b` necessarily improve performance, or can **insufficient pre-training (P)** and **under-refined alignment (A)** negate the parameter advantage?\\n\\nWe frame the question as three falsifiable claims:\\n\\n- **H1 (capacity).** With matched pre-training budget *and* matched alignment regime, 70B outperforms 13B on every capability benchmark.\\n- **H2 (pre-training dilution).** At a fixed alignment regime, reducing pre-training token budget below the 70B's compute-optimal frontier erodes the 70B advantage and may flip the ranking on hard-knowledge benchmarks.\\n- **H3 (alignment parity).** With matched pre-training budget, an `A_rlhf` 13B can match or exceed a base `A_base` 70B on instruction-following. Combined with H2, this is the regime where size \\\"loses\\\".\\n\\nA 2 \\u00d7 3 \\u00d7 3 factorial with locked defaults provides a single design that can confirm, refute, or partially support each.\\n\\n## 2. Design \\u2014 locked factorial\\n\\nIndependent variables and levels:\\n\\n| Factor | Levels | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **S \\u2014 Size** | `13B`, `70B` | LLaMA-2 architecture, shared 32k BPE tokenizer |\\n| **P \\u2014 Pre-training token budget** | `50B`, `200B`, `2T` | Continued-training-from-release on 70B; from-scratch on 13B (Chinchilla bridging) |\\n| **A \\u2014 Alignment regime** | `A_base`, `A_sft`, `A_rlhf` | `A_base` is the base LM; `A_sft` is one SFT pass; `A_rlhf` is SFT + one PPO iteration with a frozen shared RM |\\n\\nCell count: 2 \\u00d7 3 \\u00d7 3 = **18 cells**.\\n\\nChinchilla (Hoffmann et al. 2022) compute-optimal anchor, characterizing each cell:\\n\\n- Chinchilla-optimal token budgets: \\u2248260B for 13B-style models; \\u22481.4T for 70B-style models.\\n- Per-cell position relative to the frontier (frontier \\u2248 N_params \\u00d7 20 tokens, non-embedding params):\\n\\n| Cell | Tokens | Frontier (\\u2248) | Position |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 13B@50B | 50B | ~260B | **far-below** (~0.19\\u00d7) |\\n| 13B@200B | 200B | ~260B | near (~0.77\\u00d7) |\\n| 13B@2T | 2T | ~260B | **far-above** (~7.7\\u00d7) |\\n| 70B@50B | 50B | ~1.4T | **far-below** (~0.036\\u00d7, essentially untrained) |\\n| 70B@200B | 200B | ~1.4T | **far-below** (~0.14\\u00d7) |\\n| 70B@2T | 2T | ~1.4T | near-to-above (~1.43\\u00d7) |\\n\\nEach cell in the per-benchmark table in \\u00a76 carries this annotation. The reader can read off directly which cells sit *below* the compute-optimal frontier (i.e., token-limited) and which sit *above* (compute-limited / over-trained).\\n\\n## 3. Pre-training budgets \\u2014 locked execution rule\\n\\n- **70B side (default): continued-training from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b-hf`.** The release weights represent the 2T reference. For `P_low` (50B) and `P_mid` (200B) on the 70B side, continued pre-training *does not implement \\\"less pre-training\\\"*, because LLaMA-2 intermediates are not released. We therefore procure the closest open LLaMA-2-70B-equivalent with intermediates exposed \\u2014 default candidates: **RedPajama-INCITE-70B-suite intermediates** if/where they exist, else **openLLaMA-70B-equivalent**. Discrepancy from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b` is documented as a known confound (\\u00a75).\\n- **13B side (default): from-scratch at 50B / 200B / 2T tokens on the open LLaMA-2-13B config**, acting as the Chinchilla-style bridging run. This gives a clean scaling curve on the 13B side and isolates pre-training effect size.\\n- **Mixture**: both sides train on **RedPajama-v2** (or, if the researcher prefers, **FineWeb-Edu**). Tokenizer is the LLaMA-2 32k BPE. The mixture slice is shared across all P cells; only token count varies.\\n\\nThis decision rule pre-empts the asymmetric-availability problem and makes the 13B curve the canonical reference for the scaling claim, with the 70B side using release-anchored substitution.\\n\\n## 4. Alignment regime \\u2014 locked specification\\n\\n- **A_base**: raw base LM; no fine-tuning.\\n- **A_sft**: one supervised-fine-tuning pass on a **fixed, frozen SFT mixture**. Default: **Tulu-2-SFT-mixture** (Allen AI) re-mixed with a **200k-dialogue UltraChat** subset, fixed proportion 60/40. Single SFT training run per (S, P) cell; **2 seeds** per cell.\\n- **A_rlhf**: `A_sft` + **one** PPO iteration with rejection sampling. Default:\\n  - Preference data: **UltraFeedback** (Cai et al. 2023, ~64k pairs), augmented with **HH-RLHF-Anthropic** (~170k pairs) at 60/40 mix. Frozen.\\n  - Reward model: **one shared RM trained once** on the same preference mix, base = LLaMA-2-7B, fixed recipe. The RM is **frozen across all cells** so `A_rlhf` reflects only PPO procedure, not designer drift. Single reward head; no online distillation.\\n  - RM is held fixed across all (S, P) cells, which is the explicit control requested.\\n  - 1 seed per cell; **PPO policy-LR and KL coefficient are fixed constants**, identical across all cells.\\n  - Number of PPO steps is held identical across cells; we **do not** add additional iterations to the 70B cells, which would conflate wall-clock with procedure.\\n\\nIf an off-the-shelf RM is preferred: **Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B** is the named fallback, with the caveat that it is not in the LLaMA-2 family (a documented distributional shift; we treat it as a sensitivity analysis, not the default).\\n\\n## 5. Controls and confounds \\u2014 locked\\n\\n- **Architecture / tokenizer**: identical LLaMA-2 family across sizes; shared 32k BPE tokenizer.\\n- **Pre-training data**: identical mixture across all P cells; only token count varies.\\n- **Reward model**: one shared, frozen RM across all RLHF cells.\\n- **SFT data and PPO hyper-parameters**: fixed per cell.\\n- **Prompt template / chat template**: one fixed template per benchmark, and one chat template applied uniformly across sizes for any given A (per \\u00a76).\\n- **Decoding (locked)**:\\n  - MC / likelihood: **greedy** (temperature 0).\\n  - Generation (HumanEval, GSM8K-CoT, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval, IFEval): single, documented config \\u2014 `temperature = 0.7`, `top_p = 0.95`, `max_new_tokens` per task (256 HumanEval, 512 GSM8K, 1024 MT-Bench), `seed = 0`, fixed stop tokens per benchmark.\\n  - All cells use the identical config.\\n- **Contamination audit**: pre-training corpus filtered against benchmark prompts (n-gram match + minhash) where feasible; contamination report attached.\\n- **Training instability**: monitor loss spikes / NaNs / entropy collapse throughout pre-training; report as a covariate. Under-trained 70B is expected to be the most unstable cell.\\n- **Statistical variability**: \\u22652 seeds for A_sft, \\u22651 for A_rlhf per (S, P) cell; deterministic for A_base (the checkpoint is the seed).\\n- **Bootstrap 95% CIs**: applied to all stochastic-output metrics (validation perplexity, HumanEval pass@k, GSM8K pass@1, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval LC, IFEval, refusals).\\n- **Wilson CIs**: applied to bounded MC metrics (MMLU, ARC, etc.) where each question is a Bernoulli trial.\\n- **Compute covariate**: GPU-hours per cell recorded; reported, not controlled (correlates with S).\\n- **Chinchilla covariate**: token-to-frontier ratio per cell, reported in every table (see \\u00a72).\\n- **Known unresolved confounds (acknowledged, not silenced)**:\\n  70B `P_low` / `P_mid` use a continued-training or open-recipe substitute that is not byte-identical to `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b`; this is documented and analysed as a sensitivity check.\\n\\n## 6. Evaluation suite \\u2014 locked harness, templates, decoding\\n\\n**Harness**: **lm-eval-harness** (EleutherAI), pinned at a specific git tag (default: `v0.4.5`). Generation benchmarks not covered by lm-eval-harness are run using a stable wrapper around `vllm`/`transformers` (pinned) that reuses the harness's prompt templates where possible.\\n\\n**One fixed prompt template per benchmark**, taken from the harness defaults and pinned. No benchmark-level template variations across cells.\\n\\n**Benchmarks**:\\n\\n| Block | Benchmark | Decoding | Purpose |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Pre-training diag. | held-out validation perplexity (LLaMA-2-mixture slice) | n/a | sanity for P manipulation |\\n| Capability | MMLU (5-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | knowledge |\\n| Capability | ARC-Challenge (25-shot), ARC-Easy | greedy / loglikelihood | reasoning |\\n| Capability | HellaSwag (10-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | commonsense |\\n| Capability | WinoGrande (5-shot) | greedy / loglikelihood | commonsense |\\n| Capability | BoolQ, OpenBookQA | greedy / loglikelihood | reading |\\n| Capability | GSM8K (8-shot CoT) | sampling (config above) | reasoning |\\n| Capability | HumanEval (pass@1, pass@10) | sampling (config above) | code |\\n| Instruction-following | IFEval (strict and loose) | sampling | instruction-following |\\n| Chat quality | MT-Bench (single judge config) | sampling | chat |\\n| Chat quality | AlpacaEval 2.0 LC-winrate | sampling + pinned judge | chat |\\n| Honesty | TruthfulQA (MC + generation) | greedy / sampling | honesty |\\n| Bias | BBQ | greedy | bias |\\n| Safety | AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench | sampling | safety |\\n\\nJudge model for MT-Bench and AlpacaEval is **pinned and named** (default: `gpt-4-turbo-2024-04-09` for MT-Bench; same for AlpacaEval). Judge version is constant across all cells.\\n\\n## 7. Primary metric and reporting plan\\n\\nReporting is split by purpose to avoid spurious aggregation:\\n\\n- **Primary capability composite (CC)**: unweighted mean of standardised scores on MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, GSM8K, HumanEval pass@1. We standardise each metric against a fixed reference (release P_full@A_base 13B scores per-benchmark). Reported with bootstrap 95% CI.\\n- **Per-benchmark table**: every benchmark listed in \\u00a76 reported cell-by-cell with 95% CI; **safety, instruction-following, and chat-quality are NOT blended into CC** and are reported in separate tables.\\n- **Operating-point table**: each cell annotated with Chinchilla position (above / near / below frontier), inference compute (FLOPs/token), training compute (GPU-hours), and instability flags.\\n- **Directed contrasts (\\u00a72)**: each reported with effect size and adjusted p-value (Holm across the four contrasts).\\n\\nThis satisfies the researcher's directive: \\\"per-benchmark table plus an unweighted capability composite, with safety and instruction-following reported separately rather than blended in.\\\"\\n\\n## 8. Analysis plan (frozen)\\n\\nFor each metric `y`:\\n\\n1. `y ~ S + P + A + S\\u00d7P + S\\u00d7A + P\\u00d7A + S\\u00d7P\\u00d7A` linear (or beta-regression for bounded) model; report partial \\u03b7\\u00b2 for each term.\\n2. Compute the four contrasts from \\u00a72 with mean-difference estimators and bootstrap 95% CIs; Holm-correct across the four.\\n3. Per-cell tables with CIs, Chinchilla tag, instability tag.\\n4. Plots: capability metric vs P for each (S, A); overlay the four contrasts.\\n5. Sensitivity check: rerun the four contrasts on the 70B side substituting the open-recipe weights if intermediates are not from `meta-llama/Llama-2-70b`.\\n\\nPre-registered failure conditions:\\n\\n- If 70B@P_low@A_base is more unstable than `1 SD-loss-spike-rate` above 13B@P_low@A_base, the 70B@P_low cells are flagged unstable and reported separately. We do not silently drop them.\\n- If contamination audit flags >1% benchmark prompt overlap in any cell's training data, that cell is excluded from CC; the conflict is reported.\\n\\n## 9. Feasibility and execution notes\\n\\n- **In this sandbox**: not executable. No GPU, no network, no datasets, no project dependencies. The deliverable here is the design only. No run is claimed; no fabricated numbers appear above. Every cell exists as a configuration.\\n- **In a real environment**:\\n  - 70B continued-training from release, 50B tokens: a single 32\\u00d7H100 (80 GB) node \\u00d7 ~2\\u20134 days (~3,000\\u20136,000 GPU-hours).\\n  - 70B continued-training from release, 200B tokens: ~12,000\\u201324,000 GPU-hours.\\n  - 70B SFT \\u00d7 2 seeds: ~1,500 GPU-hours per (S, P) cell on the 70B side; double-counted for the 4 (P \\u00d7 2-cell) primary 70B P sweep \\u21d2 a few thousand GPU-hours.\\n  - 70B PPO \\u00d7 1 iter (A_rlhf): ~2,000\\u20135,000 GPU-hours per (S, P) cell.\\n  - 13B from-scratch at 50B / 200B / 2T: dominated by 2T at ~80k\\u2013120k GPU-hours on 13B; the 2T run is the budget bottleneck on the 13B side. **Bridging alternative**: 13B from-scratch at 50B / 200B only, with 13B@2T supplied by `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-hf` and explicitly flagged as not-from-scratch (saves most of the cost). The 70B at \\\"2T\\\" is similarly anchored to the release weights and the same flag applies.\\n- **Storage**: 70B FP16 \\u2248 140 GB per checkpoint; 18 cells \\u00d7 2 (best + last) \\u2248 5 TB of checkpoints. Mitigation: keep best checkpoint per cell + final SFT/PPO iter only; intermediate training-state checkpoints are not retained.\\n- **Inference compute for generation benchmarks**: roughly 1\\u20132 GPU-day per cell on the 70B side at MT-Bench scale; negligible for likelihood benchmarks.\\n\\n## 10. Limitations explicitly flagged (not papered over)\\n\\n1. **Continued-training vs from-scratch asymmetry**: 13B is from-scratch; 70B is release-anchored. Discussed in \\u00a73 and \\u00a75.\\n2. **Open-recipe substitute for 70B intermediates**: if RedPajama / openLLaMA 70B intermediates are used in lieu of LLaMA-2 intermediates, the curve is not strictly LLaMA-2. Sensitivity check defined in \\u00a78.\\n3. **Single PPO iteration**: with one PPO step, `A_rlhf` cell differences are small in absolute terms; the design is sensitive to this. Documented as a deliberate choice to isolate the *procedure effect* rather than to chase state-of-the-art.\\n4. **No closed-source data**: the design uses only public corpora and frozen open RM/SFT/RM-training recipes.\\n5. **Compute bookkeeping**: GPU-hours is reported as a covariate, not an independent variable; size and compute remain partially confounded. The Chinchilla-relative annotation makes this visible.\\n6. **Judge model drift**: MT-Bench/AlpacaEval numbers depend on the judge. We pin a single judge; absolute scores are not directly comparable to other studies, but the *relative* ordering across our 18 cells remains internal-valid.\\n\\n## 11. Bottom-line answers the design supports\\n\\nThe factorial is built to support a precise, falsifiable bottom-line readout:\\n\\n- *If `70B@P_full@A_base - 13B@P_full@A_base > 0` on CC* \\u2014 capacity hypothesis (H1) partly held; inspect per-benchmark for where it fails.\\n- *If `70B@P_low@A_base \\u2264 13B@P_full@A_base` on hard-knowledge benchmarks* \\u2014 pre-training dilution hypothesis (H2) supported.\\n- *If `13B@P_full@A_rlhf - 70B@P_full@A_base > 0` on IFEval and MT-Bench* \\u2014 alignment-parity hypothesis (H3) supported.\\n- *If `70B@P_low@A_rlhf` matches `13B@P_full@A_base` on capability and beats it on instruction-following* \\u2014 the most direct case for \\\"size loses\\\" when both factors are constrained.\\n\\nEach of these is a pre-registered success/failure criterion with a defined computed statistic.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*End of design. No experiments have been run. No execution is claimed.*\\n\", \"evidence\": \"Repository `/workspace` contains a single file, `task.json` (sha256 `4f5a54b3501ea9039e8dd00c8cc49bb0a82e80eb1f79520fbbbb13b73e2c36bf`); no source code, datasets, scripts, or prior runs are present. The sandbox has no GPU, no network, and no preinstalled dependencies, consistent with the design-only contract. The environment was inspected via `ls -la /workspace` and `sha256sum /workspace/task.json`; no code execution related to the experiment was performed and no results are reported anywhere in this document.\", \"assumptions\": [\"The released llama-2-13b and llama-2-70b base checkpoints (\\u22482T tokens, Meta, 2023) serve as the canonical \\\"fully pre-trained\\\" reference for those sizes.\", \"Continued pre-training from the released llama-2-70b is an acceptable practical proxy for \\\"less pre-training\\\" on the 70B side, with the caveat documented in Limitations.\", \"From-scratch training of LLaMA-2-13B-style models at 50B, 200B, and 2T tokens on RedPajama-v2 or FineWeb-Edu is achievable and produces a clean Chinchilla bridging curve.\", \"Tulu-2-SFT-mixture + a 200k-dialogue UltraChat subset is a faithful stand-in for the LLaMA-2-Chat SFT data, and UltraFeedback + HH-RLHF (60/40) is a faithful stand-in for the LLaMA-2-Chat preference data.\", \"An RM trained once on UltraFeedback+HH-RLHF with a fixed recipe and frozen across all (S, P) cells adequately controls \\\"shared RM\\\" as required.\", \"lm-eval-harness (v0.4.5 default) with one fixed prompt template per benchmark, greedy decoding for likelihood and (temperature=0.7, top_p=0.95, seed=0) for generation, yields reliable cross-cell comparisons.\", \"Chinchilla's compute-optimal frontier (Hoffmann et al. 2022) at N_eff \\u00d7 20 tokens is the right reference for \\\"above/below frontier\\\" annotation.\", \"One PPO iteration per (S, P) cell under a fixed KL coefficient and policy-LR captures the A_rlhf effect of interest; multi-iteration RLHF is treated as out-of-scope.\", \"The unweighted mean of standardised capability benchmarks is a defensible primary composite; safety and instruction-following are kept separate.\", \"Public benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, BoolQ, OpenBookQA, GSM8K, HumanEval, IFEval, MT-Bench, AlpacaEval 2.0, TruthfulQA, BBQ, AdvBench, ToxicGen, HarmBench) adequately discriminate the relevant differences.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Compute budget for the actual (out-of-environment) execution; specifically, whether the 13B@2T cell can really be done from-scratch or must be anchored to `meta-llama/Llama-2-13b-hf`.\", \"Availability of LLaMA-2-class 70B intermediates from the RedPajama / openLLaMA / Together ecosystems; if not, the design sensitivity check (\\u00a78 step 5) is the only quantitative fallback.\", \"Best public surrogate for the LLaMA-2-Chat SFT mixture (Tulu-2 vs UltraChat vs OASST) and the proportion of synthetic vs human-curated data.\", \"RM choice \\u2014 train-a-shared-RM-from-scratch vs use Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B as a frozen out-of-family RM.\", \"Judge model selection for MT-Bench / AlpacaEval and the licensing constraints on commercial judges in the actual execution environment.\", \"Whether the contamination audit (n-gram + minhash) at >1% threshold is the right operating point.\", \"Whether one PPO iteration is enough signal on the 70B side; if not, multi-iteration RLHF may bias A_rlhf in size-dependent ways.\", \"Boundary of \\\"near frontier\\\" in the Chinchilla annotation \\u2014 currently \\u00b125% subjectively; a precise rule can be added if the researcher specifies.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-89dba723-87dc-4171-9703-1b5afa3ae7d3", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 17}
